{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G1qMGT2B4mmwyrbXDxj4pv","type":"tool_result","content":"1690\t<p><strong>A:</strong> Yes, they're distinctly different dishes:</p>\n1691\t<ul>\n1692\t  <li><strong>Noodles:</strong> Laksa Johor uses thick spaghetti; Laksa Lemak uses thin rice noodles.</li>\n1693\t  <li><strong>Broth:</strong> Laksa Johor is heavier and more coconut-forward; Laksa Lemak is more balanced with more visible spices.</li>\n1694\t  <li><strong>Fish content:</strong> Laksa Johor emphasizes fresh or salted fish; Laksa Lemak uses ground fish paste.</li>\n1695\t  <li><strong>Texture:</strong> Laksa Johor is creamier and richer; Laksa Lemak is lighter and more delicate.</li>\n1696\t  <li><strong>Regional pride:</strong> Johor locals consider Laksa Lemak a different (inferior) dish. Don't confuse them.</li>\n1697\t</ul>\n1698\t\n1699\t<p><strong>Q: Where should I try it first?</strong></p>\n1700\t<p><strong>A:</strong> If staying at <a href=\"/rooms\">Pelangi Capsule Hostel</a>, walk to Laksa Auntie Nur in Taman Pelangi—closest and most consistent. For a broader JB experience, visit Larkin Terminal stall during lunch hours (11am–2pm) when the broth is freshest.</p>\n1701\t\n1702\t<p><strong>Q: Is it very spicy?</strong></p>\n1703\t<p><strong>A:</strong> The broth itself is mild. Heat comes from the sambal paste served on the side. Add it gradually. Most stalls offer a milder version if you ask (\"sedikit pedas\" = a little spicy).</p>\n1704\t\n1705\t<h2>When to Visit & Best Times</h2>\n1706\t<p>Laksa Johor is available year-round but best during lunch (11am–2pm) and early dinner (5–7pm) when the broth is freshest. Many stalls close by 8–9pm. Weekday lunch is less crowded than weekends; come early to secure a seat at popular stalls.</p>\n1707\t\n1708\t<h2>Next Steps</h2>\n1709\t<p>Now that you know what to look for, <a href=\"/book\">book a stay at Pelangi Capsule</a> and make Laksa Johor one of your first meals in the city. It's a dish that defines Johor's food culture.</p>\n1710\t`,\n1711\t  },\n1712\t];\n1713\t\n1714\texport const blogPosts: BlogPost[] = rawPosts.map((post) => ({\n1715\t  ...post,\n1716\t  readingTimeMinutes: computeReadingTime(post.content),\n1717\t}));\n1718\t\n1719\texport function getPostBySlug(slug: string): BlogPost | undefined {\n1720\t  return blogPosts.find((post) => post.slug === slug);\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9d69bad7-fd70-4f14-8ebe-891ae4eab84a","uuid":"dbe08b5e-7dc9-42e7-871f-92db78280956","timestamp":"2026-04-14T11:21:41.884Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\site\\src\\data\\blog\\posts.ts","content":"<p><strong>A:</strong> Yes, they're distinctly different dishes:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Noodles:</strong> Laksa Johor uses thick spaghetti; Laksa Lemak uses thin rice noodles.</li>\n  <li><strong>Broth:</strong> Laksa Johor is heavier and more coconut-forward; Laksa Lemak is more balanced with more visible spices.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fish content:</strong> Laksa Johor emphasizes fresh or salted fish; Laksa Lemak uses ground fish paste.</li>\n  <li><strong>Texture:</strong> Laksa Johor is creamier and richer; Laksa Lemak is lighter and more delicate.</li>\n  <li><strong>Regional pride:</strong> Johor locals consider Laksa Lemak a different (inferior) dish. Don't confuse them.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Q: Where should I try it first?</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A:</strong> If staying at <a href=\"/rooms\">Pelangi Capsule Hostel</a>, walk to Laksa Auntie Nur in Taman Pelangi—closest and most consistent. For a broader JB experience, visit Larkin Terminal stall during lunch hours (11am–2pm) when the broth is freshest.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Q: Is it very spicy?</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A:</strong> The broth itself is mild. Heat comes from the sambal paste served on the side. Add it gradually. Most stalls offer a milder version if you ask (\"sedikit pedas\" = a little spicy).</p>\n\n<h2>When to Visit & Best Times</h2>\n<p>Laksa Johor is available year-round but best during lunch (11am–2pm) and early dinner (5–7pm) when the broth is freshest. Many stalls close by 8–9pm. Weekday lunch is less crowded than weekends; come early to secure a seat at popular stalls.</p>\n\n<h2>Next Steps</h2>\n<p>Now that you know what to look for, <a href=\"/book\">book a stay at Pelangi Capsule</a> and make Laksa Johor one of your first meals in the city. It's a dish that defines Johor's food culture.</p>\n`,\n  },\n];\n\nexport const blogPosts: BlogPost[] = rawPosts.map((post) => ({\n  ...post,\n  readingTimeMinutes: computeReadingTime(post.content),\n}));\n\nexport function getPostBySlug(slug: string): BlogPost | undefined {\n  return blogPosts.find((post) => post.slug === slug);","numLines":31,"startLine":1690,"totalLines":1722}}}
